  "query_preview": "/Tuesday through Saturday/...\nThe weather pattern through at least the middle of the week will \ncontinue to support good moisture levels over at least the eastern \nhalf of Arizona and likely improved upper level ascent for Tuesday-\nThursday. Tuesday may involve a brief period of drier air moving \ninto south-central Arizona from the southwest and this shows up in \nthe fairly limited PoPs Tuesday afternoon/evening. However, NBM \nguidance shows a resurgence of moisture and rain chances during the \novernight hours Tuesday night/Wednesday morning. Chances Wednesday \ninto Thursday are also looking better with a potential disturbance \ntracking northward into central/eastern Arizona at some point. \nNeedless to say, the model differences call into question the best \ntiming for rain chances and the exact areas, but as long as the \nupper level support and good moisture remain present chances will \nremain elevated. \n\nWPC has added a slight risk on the Days 2-4 (Monday- Wednesday) \nExcessive Rainfall Outlooks over the eastern Arizona high terrain. \nIt would not be surprising for subsequent issuance's of the EROs to \nextend the risk through Thursday or even Friday depending on the \nforecast available moisture. Although the risk for heavy rainfall \nand flooding will be present through at least the middle of the \nweek, the risk for strong downdraft/outflow winds is likely to \ndecrease with guidance showing decreasing forecast DCAPEs. Models \nare still trending toward drier air finally moving eastward through \nmost of Arizona by Friday or Saturday. This should mostly shut down \nrain chances over the lower deserts and decrease chances over higher \nterrain areas.\n\nTemperatures throughout the entire week are expected to remain \nfairly close to seasonal normals with the lowest forecast highs \noccurring on Tuesday and Wednesday. As long as models are correct \nwith the drying trend late in the week into next weekend, \ntemperatures are favored to drift back to above normal with highs \nmore in a 103-108 degree range by Friday or Saturday.",
